tes in  the united states on a year-to-year  basis have been declining that is  the first fact we have to introduce here  because the entire conversation stems  from that  despite the fact that we have more and  more people living in the united states  over time  actual crime rates for things like  violent crime robbery property crimes  it has all been declining on a per  household basis  when you survey people and you ask them  do you  think crime has increased in the united  states from last year  almost every year more than 50 percent  of the people in the united states  believe that yes  crime has actually gone up so crime has  been going down  since the early 90s people mostly  believe  crime has been going up okay that's step  one  now there are a whole bunch of other  details and  complications that warrant some more  exploration so let's do a couple of them  before we move on  for example some types of crime might be  up one year  even in the midst of a downward trend or  some types of crime might be up one year  even when other types of crime are down  so as an example  in 2020 murder is up slightly but a  whole bunch of other crimes  are down and over time all of these  types of violent crime  are down so you would have to decide can  i really make any relevant observation  in one year about well murder's up  but rape and robbery are down and arson  is up but  burglary and sexual assault or whatever  right so  overall crime rates have been declining  for a long time and they continue to  decline  it's also important to remember that not  all crimes are reported to police so  anytime we're looking at something that  depends on reporting to  an authority you have to consider that  the records  may not reflect reality at equal rates  so as an example which many on the right  don't like  sexual assault is notoriously  under-reported  however that doesn't really fit into  their narrative if they want to make it  seem as though crime in general is  under-reported you really have to start  with sexual assault which they don't  seem particularly willing to do  then you have other crimes crimes like  vandalism and public intoxication  which arguably should not even really be  a crime which  certainly are visible in that you see  graffiti or a broken window  or with whatever the case may be but  those are not the serious violent crimes  that we're really mostly talking about  when we talk about how police should be  reformed  so that gets us to what's going on today  since the george floyd protests have  started  we've been hearing uh defund the police  we've been hearing abolish the police  we've been hearing reform  we've been hearing just fund the police  blindly give them as much money as they  want we've heard every combination of  things  the majority of the left including joe  biden  is talking about reform uh it could  include reallocation of funds taking  funds away maybe from militarization  while  increasing funds for training as one  example but  it doesn't really matter in terms of the  crime we're seeing now because remember  all of the discussions about defunding  abolishing restructuring  all that stuff it's in the future it  hasn't happened yet and yet still you  have people like donald trump himself  and his acolytes tweeting videos of  things going on now  in uh you know portland or chicago  saying  this is the future of the country if joe  biden is elected president  hold on a second first of all that's  trump's america right now  so to say this is what's going on now  and this is  what would be happening even more under  joe biden you then have to see  does what is it that joe biden would do  that would exacerbate this and there  actually isn't anything  but it also ignores that none of these  abolish or defund things have become a  reality so  they are arguing we are seeing more  crime in cities  because police have been defunded by  democrats the reality is crime continues  to go down  and nothing's been defunded yet there  have been some conversations in certain  places about  how might we reduce funding to police in  some places  you know northampton massachusetts is a  place where the show started where  there's been this discussion  there's been a decrease to the rate at  which police funding will  increase if that makes sense um but  all of this stuff is in the future so  you certainly  can't blame a future  possible changes to police funding for  current increases in crime particularly  when there have been no such increases  today  there have been discussions about do we  change who responds to certain types of  911  when they relate to homelessness or  mental illness as an example okay  those discussions have been had there  are some places that have started to  look at making some of those changes but  these policies haven't yet been put in  place when it comes to defund or abolish  i don't think that they are going to and  joe biden certainly doesn't  uh support them so when you look at this  broader context  you do have to ask why are people  falling for the idea that crime is up  and this actually takes me back to my  days as an undergrad at the university  of massachusetts amherst  where uh michael morgan who's been a  guest on this program  studied this extensively eric ashar  also studied this and both of those  interviews are on the youtube channel  there's often this assumption and we see  it after mass shootings we see it after  a lot of things  that violence in the media and in  popular culture in movies and video  games  makes people act violently in the real  world  now by and large that doesn't seem to be  true  it if you look at the scholarship on  this it's certainly possible that people  already predisposed to be violent in the  real world  might be triggered to act um in a  particular way  based on things they consume in violent  video games or movies but that's a small  small portion of the population by and  large we have not  found that violent video games or  watching violent news or  or whatever makes people violent in the  new in the real world  however what does seem to be the case is  that seeing  violence on the news and in media may  make  people more afraid and it makes people  believe  that the world is more dangerous than it  is and in fact  when you look at local news and you  realize wow murder is down over the last  30 years all of these violent crimes are  down over the last 30 years  but the representation in media is  identical or has even grown because of  this if it bleeds  it leads ideology it makes sense that  based on watching news people would  believe the world is more dangerous than  it is people would believe there is more  crime  than there actually is so what you have  right now  in part thanks to the trump  administration uh  is this use it's all based on getting  trump reelected and we're going to deal  with that a little bit more later in the  show  you have a lot of discussion of very  rare  individual incidents that are happening  in the shadows of mostly peaceful  protests but the takeaway we have to  remember  is that crime rates have been on the  decline since the 90s and there  certainly has been no defunding of the  police yet  which you could blame for non-existent  crime increases that they claim exist  now one other thing that i think is  important to think about  and that's the war on drugs and prison  populations  some will say clearly there is more  crime  because people are in prison for  committing crimes more than ever before  there are more people in prison right  now so clearly there's more crime going  on  just because there are more people in  prison doesn't mean that the streets of  the united states are more dangerous  from a violent crime  standpoint if you add things to the list  of stuff that will get you put in prison  you would expect that more people are  going to end up in prison  if you put in place mandatory minimums  or if judicial discretion is used  to sentence people to longer prison  sentences  you can see prisons more full even  if violent crime is on the decline in  general society  which it is so it's really important not  to fall for that propaganda  because at every level there there is to  go back to the beginning there is a gish  gallop going on  where a whole bunch of stuff is thrown  out crime is up  and it's crime because it's up because  police have been defunded and joe biden  would do more of that  you've got to break it down is crime up  no uh  have police been defunded already in a  way that would even relate to what we're  seeing right now  no does joe biden want to defund the  police if  if he becomes president no every single  one  of the premises is wrong and they count  on  because they don't have the facts on  their side side they count on you not  knowing the facts  or you not checking the facts and ending  up having to argue on the merits and  when we accept the facts  and debate what should be done we're  missing the fact that the premises are  all wrong hopefully this is useful when  we see  everything from don jr's tweets to  discussions in right-wing media  and on the internet about these issues  go and look at whether the premises make  sense and check out in this case  they don't starting with crime has been  on a downward trend  for decades in the united states let me  know what you think i'm on twitter at  deepakman  we have a lot happening this week this  is the month  of the 15-year anniversary of the david  pakman show can you imagine that and  i'll talk to you later on in the month  about  what we're going to do the exact date is  in the second half of the month  uh but a very very exciting month we'll  take a quick break and be back right  after this  if you love feeding your intellectual  curiosity but you're always  struggling to find the time check out  one of my all-time  favorite apps called blinkist blinkist  lets you consume an entire book  on your way home from work they take  thousands of popular nonfiction books  condense each one into text or audio  that you can get through  in just 15 minutes and you get all of  the important  core ideas and information from that  book it's perfect if you don't have  enough time  to do all the reading you want to do or  if you just want to sample a book  before you buy the entire thing i  recently read a brief history of time of  course by the great  stephen hawking this is a book that i  have been aware of for so long  and other things got in the way and it  was fantastic to check it out on  blinkist  blinkist has books on politics  philosophy science they have 27  different non-fiction categories and a  subscription is only about eight bucks a  month  and you get the entire library but  you can try it totally free and get 25  off a subscription  when you go to blinkist.com pacman  i've put the link right underneath this  video

